From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 16:15:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11323/11362] Correction: the conductor fixed the cascade I
 said nothing would re-raise

At 19:15Z I recorded that the exp7025 cascade had rolled out of view unfixed and
that nothing would re-raise it until some future task gated on a live trace. The
planner re-raised it 34 minutes later: .616 ran the snapshot-to-blob model
identity bridge at 19:49Z, and arc_eval_provenance.py now resolves the snapshot
revision from the requested path instead of demanding a bare *.gguf basename.

The reasoning error is the keeper. I inferred the forcing function was gone from
one observation -- the cascade line disappearing on milestone activation -- without
asking whether the planner reads the failure record independently of whether the
blocked task is still queued. It does, and conductor-log.md carries the rows.

All day I have caught instruments reporting the absence of a symptom as the
absence of a problem. Here I read the absence of a symptom as the absence of the
system's repair capability. Same question in both directions: what else, besides
the thing I am watching, carries this signal?

Also records that the substrate-class gate merged in ac3f5a81ac is live in
_verify_artifact_impl and has not run once. The pre-merge check was a standalone
call; the conductor's path through _log_experiment_completion is a deployment
test that has not happened.

TWO mutation markers were deleted deliberately, each with evidence, neither by
habit. Both named a file the arming run did not write, tagged [NOT this run].

The first named ops/dogfood-memory.json: a single counter increment, 5347 to 5348,
in a file whose last five commits are all [conductor], which writes it on nearly
every task. Routine conductor state misattributed to a pytest run.

The second named ops/status.md -- this commit's own authored content -- armed by
the conductor's smart-subset pre-test run while the edit sat dirty. git checkout
on that path would have destroyed the correction being committed.

Both are the inherent over-reporting the guard's own documentation names: it diffs
a shared working tree against a baseline and cannot tell whose write it was.
Deleting a marker touches no file; reverting the paths it lists is the destruction
this guard has caused three times.

